(top starts / sleepers / busts / injuries / full ranking table), which is how a reader actually queries them.\n\nShared entity notes (one note, blocks from several documents): `nfl_week_14_running_back_injury_report` (doc_0198 + doc_0317 \u2014 the same Henry/Stevenson/Walker/Jones/Foreman/Swift/Taylor injuries reported in the standard and PPR editions), `fantasy_rb_week_14_bye_week_impact` (same pair), `superdraft_week_14_nfl_dfs_lineup` (doc_0276 + doc_0331 publish an identical lineup), `superdraft_dfs_multiplier_scoring_format` (doc_0056 + doc_0276 + doc_0331), `fanduel_nfl_dfs_contest_format_and_scoring` (doc_0056 + doc_0276), `erik_ten_hag_under_pressure_december_2023` (doc_0129 + doc_0315), and `man_united_2023_24_champions_league_elimination` (doc_0187 Group A + doc_0315). Writing these once rather than twice is what lets a single note carry evidence from two publishers' framings of the same fact.\n\nWhat was dropped and why. Almost all of it is structural chrome: article titles (carried in each note's H1), section headers, the \"MORE:\"/\"WEEK 14 FANTASY ADVICE:\"/\"QBs | RBs | WRs\" cross-link rails that recur in every fantasy piece, and sportsbook promo lines (\"Win BIG with SuperDraft! Get a free $10 deposit\"). doc_0034 accounts for the largest single-document drop (9.1%) because the live blog embeds each tweet twice \u2014 the \"\ud83d\udcfa: #KCvsGB on NBC / \ud83d\udcf1: Stream on #NFLPlus\" pairs are pure embed furniture. I deliberately kept duplicated *captions* (doc_0034 blocks 79 and 88, repeated Taylor Swift/Kelce lines) rather than discard them as duplicates, since they carry content. I also assigned several blocks that read like headers but state a claim: doc_0005 block 62 (the prediction headline), doc_0258 blocks 14/16/18/20 (each names a scenario condition, not just a label), and doc_0034 block 10 (names the venue, Lambeau Field). The one place I dropped a plausible-looking content block is doc_0258 blocks 30/32 (\"Alabama strength of schedule\" / \"strength of record\") \u2014 the ESPN figures they head are fully carried in blocks 31 and 33.\n\nScope conditions worth preserving when these notes are written. Several documents state something that contradicts or narrows their own headline, and the note author must not smooth it over: doc_0034's headline is a Packers win, but its lead paragraph and blocks 2/4/5/16/18 argue the finish was decided by officiating error \u2014 hence a separate `argument` note rather than folding it into the result. doc_0197 blocks 29 and 30 are late \"UPDATE:\" lines that reverse the injury status given earlier in the same piece (Douglas ruled OUT; Kirk downgraded from questionable to doubtful) \u2014 they belong in the injury note, not the rankings table. doc_0100 block 15 carries the competition's load-bearing exception (\"no extra time up to and including the quarter-finals \u2026 straight to penalties\"), and block 16 the counter-exception for semifinal second legs and the final. doc_0202 block 3 lists the promo codes whose terms blocks 7-35 then qualify by state (BetRivers pays $100, $250 or $500 depending on jurisdiction; DraftKings' 20% match needs a $5,000 deposit and carries 25x playthrough).\n\nGenre caveat on coverage. doc_0005, doc_0056, doc_0276 and doc_0331 land in the 90-92% band because each is partly a promotional vehicle \u2014 affiliate rails, \"use promo code TSN\" lines, and repeated cross-sell headers. Nothing substantive was discarded from them. doc_0202 is the most promotional document in the cluster (seven of its eight notes are sportsbook offer terms), but it scores 96% because those terms are themselves the content; only its title and two section headers are chrome.",
